{
 "id": "three-blind-mice",
 "version": 1,
 "reviewed": true,
 "title": "Three Blind Mice (round)",
 "description": "The nursery round in four parts, as a Boston songbook of 1906 printed it, for 2 to 4 singers: everyone sings the same tune twice, entering four bars apart (tenor and bass an octave lower), and the parts tumble over each other. The words are the 1906 ones.",
 "language": "en",
 "difficulty": "beginner",
 "genre": [
  "round",
  "children's",
  "folk"
 ],
 "style": "straight",
 "tempo": 72,
 "key": "C",
 "timeSignature": "6/8",
 "pickupBeats": 0,
 "credits": {
  "words": "traditional",
  "tune": "traditional, as printed in Home Songs (Oliver Ditson, 1906)",
  "arrangement": "Stackapella (round setting)"
 },
 "licence": {
  "words": "public domain: traditional, printed 1906 with no editor named",
  "tune": "public domain: traditional, printed 1906 with no editor named (and by H. F. Sefton, Toronto, 1869)",
  "arrangement": "original round setting. Accepted exceptions (singable.py): a round: every part sings the 1906 tune itself, entering four bars apart (tenor and bass an octave lower), so its rhythm stands: a quaver's rest after each \"mice,\" and the first \"run!\", and none before the pickups \"They\" and \"Did\" after the second \"run!\" and \"knife:\", which leaves no full breath for the length of the round; the tune's own low C on \"mice,\" and \"mice.\" a tone under the beginner soprano range; the bass, an octave under the tune, reaching C and B above its range on \"all ran af-ter the farm-\", \"cut off their tails with a carv-\" and \"ev-er you see such a sight\"; and the B's doubled where two parts sing the same bar of the tune together, \"see such a\" against \"af-ter the\" (beats 25, 33, 41, 49 and 57), the B a passing note in the tune's run.",
  "recordings": "synthesized guides",
  "checked": null,
  "source": "Home Songs: a collection of favorite songs, hymns and rounds for the fireside (Oliver Ditson Company, Boston, 1906), p. 95, \"Three Blind Mice (round in four parts)\", entries numbered 1 to 4 four bars apart, in D, written here a whole tone lower in C; read from the scan at archive.org/details/homesongscollect00unse_0 (leaf n100), and checked against Old Favorite Songs (Lester Piano Co., Philadelphia, 1906), p. 18. The tune as a round is also in H. F. Sefton's Three-Part Songs (Toronto, 1869; 1879 printing, p. 70)."
 },
